---
title: Upgrade generated project files
description: Review newer supplementary templates and preserve local customizations.
section: How-to guides
order: 6
updated: '2026-10-14'
---

Use this workflow after updating Rivet through your plugin manager and restarting Neovim. The project must have a `.rivet.json` manifest. Upgrade updates supplementary templates using the project's saved choices; it preserves application sources and tests.

## Review and apply

1. Open a file in the project and run `:RivetUpgrade`.
2. Review the proposed files and skipped-conflict notes. Depending on the provider, files may include editor settings, CMake helpers, presets, README guidance, or plugin help.
3. Press `c` to apply, or `q` to cancel.
4. Run the project's Build and Test tasks to check the updated configuration.

From the CLI, preview first:

```sh
rivet upgrade --root ./my_project --preview
```

Then apply:

```sh
rivet upgrade --root ./my_project
```

The second command computes a fresh plan. Keep the project unchanged between inspection and application. Neither command changes the selected language, standard, runtime, or dependency pins.

## Handle a skipped customization

Upgrade replaces a supplementary file when it still matches its recorded baseline. It can also restore a missing supplementary file or record a baseline for a file already equal to the current template.

If you edited a file, Rivet preserves it and reports the conflict. You can keep that customization. To compare newer defaults, create a separate project with the same language and settings, compare the corresponding file, and merge useful changes manually. The old baseline remains recorded, so another Upgrade does not treat your customization as an untouched template. There is no force-overwrite option.

If a file changes while the Neovim review is open, or an affected buffer has unsaved edits, application stops. Save your work and start a new review.

## Upgrade an older project

Older manifests may lack `template_baselines`. An existing file is adopted only if it matches the current template or a recorded managed snapshot; otherwise Rivet leaves it alone. Missing supplementary files can still be created. Missing application sources are not restored.

After application, `.rivet.json` records the current `rivet_version` and the baselines it could adopt. Local task settings in `.rivet.local.json` and machine-specific `CMakeUserPresets.json` settings are preserved.

## Choose another operation when needed

- To restore an edited managed file to its recorded contents, use [Repair](/docs/troubleshooting/#recover-an-edited-managed-file). Repair saves the edited copy under `.rivet-recovery/`.
- For a legacy project with `.outset.json`, first review `:RivetMigrate` or `rivet migrate --preview`. Upgrade does not perform that conversion.
- For a project without a Rivet manifest, use the [existing-project workflow](/docs/existing-projects/).
